      "expected_output": "item product=1 quantity=4 unit_price=3.20 subtotal=12.80\ntotal=12.80\ncount=4\n"
    }
  ]
}

=== feature4_update_item ===
{
  "description": "Updating an existing line item replaces its quantity and unit price with the supplied values (it does not accumulate onto the prior quantity). After an update, the cart total reflects the new unit price times the new quantity, and the cart's reported item count equals the new quantity.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "products": [
          1
        ],
        "operations": [
          {
            "op": "add",
            "product": 1,
            "unit_price": "100",
            "quantity": 3
          },
          {
            "op": "update",
            "product": 1,
            "unit_price": "200",
            "quantity": 2
          },
          {
            "op": "dump"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "item product=1 quantity=2 unit_price=200.00 subtotal=400.00\ntotal=400.00\ncount=2\n"
    }
  ]
}

And before you ask a follow-up: your replace-vs-increment worry is already settled by the feature4 file above — add(3 @ 100) then update(2 @ 200) yields count=2 and subtotal=400.00, not 5 units of accumulated values. The file is the contract; re-read it instead of asking me to paraphrase it. Too many questions. Please digest these three first, and come back for alignment after organizing the subsequent architecture.

One point, one artifact. Here is feature5_item_label, verbatim:

=== feature5_item_label ===
{
  "description": "A line item carries a human-readable label that combines its quantity with the name of the product type it references, so a cart listing can be rendered without inspecting raw fields.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "products": [
          1
        ],
        "operations": [
          {
            "op": "add",
            "product": 1,
            "unit_price": "100",
            "quantity": 3
          },
          {
            "op": "label",
            "product": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "label=3 units of User\n"
    }
  ]
}

That closes out the public set: feature1 through feature5 are all in your hands. Note the label case confirms what I already ruled — 'label' carries no quantity field, <n> is the line item's recorded quantity, and 'User' is the constructed fixture's type name, not a hardcoded string. You now have every public case file. Stop asking for what you hold and start building; next useful question from you is an architecture or edge-case proposal, not another artifact request.
